<body>
    <h1>Object Tracking</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <h2 id=3D"ObjectTracking-Introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>Object tracking in computer vision is the process of identifying and fol=
lowing the position of a single object or multiple objects across a sequenc=
e of images or video. This involves detecting the object initially in one i=
mage or frame and then continuously updating its position as the video or i=
mage sequence progresses.</p>
<p>The goal is to make sure the object is followed accurately, even when it=
 moves, changes its appearance, or interacts with other objects in the envi=
ronment.</p>
<p>Object tracking is one of the main challenges in computer vision, as it =
requires not only accurately detecting objects but also doing so robustly a=
nd efficiently across multiple images or video frames, taking into account =
factors such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Position changes</strong>: The object might move quickly or unpr=
edictably.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Appearance changes</strong>: The object might change color, shap=
e, or get occluded.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Dynamic environments</strong>: Other objects or people might com=
e into view.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"ObjectTracking-ExamplesofUseinAppleProducts">Examples of Use in A=
pple Products</h3>
<p>Apple uses object tracking techniques in various products and features t=
hrough the <strong>Vision framework and other technologies</strong> to enha=
nce user interaction and experience. Some examples include:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Augmented Reality (AR) with ARKit</strong>: ARKit, Apple=E2=80=
=99s platform for creating augmented reality experiences, uses object and s=
urface tracking to allow virtual objects to interact correctly with the rea=
l world. For example, ARKit can track the position of an object in 3D space=
 and project virtual models onto it.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>iPhone Camera (Photography and Video)</strong>: In Portrait Mode=
, Apple uses tracking to identify and isolate the face or object in focus, =
blurring the background to create a depth effect.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Face ID</strong>: Face ID, Apple=E2=80=99s facial recognition te=
chnology, uses advanced object tracking techniques to identify and track th=
e user=E2=80=99s face through a system called TrueDepth.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"ObjectTracking-HowtoImplementanObjectTracker">How to Implement an=
 Object Tracker</h2>
<p>To implement an object tracker, the first step is to give your app permi=
ssion to access the camera. You can do this by adding the "Privacy - Camera=
 Usage Description" field in the target settings.</p>
<p>Inside your app, you can check whether the required permission has been =
granted using the following code:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func che=
ckPermission() {
        switch AVCaptureDevice.authorizationStatus(for: .video) {
            // Permission has been granted before
            case .authorized:
                permissionGranted =3D true
               =20
            // Permission has not been requested yet
            case .notDetermined:
                requestPermission()
                   =20
            default:
                permissionGranted =3D false
            }
    }
   =20
    func requestPermission() {
        sessionQueue.suspend()
        AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess(for: .video) { [unowned self] granted=
 in
            self.permissionGranted =3D granted
            self.sessionQueue.resume()
        }
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>You can call this permission check at the start of your app or from the =
component that will handle tracking:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"> overrid=
e func viewDidLoad() {
        checkPermission()=20
 }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The next step is to create a detector and set up the view to use it.</p>
<p>In the view controller, we=E2=80=99ll define a variable responsible for =
capturing the images from the camera and create a session to manage the cam=
era's inputs and outputs:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">private =
let session =3D AVsession()
private let sessionQueue =3D DispatchQueue(label: "sessionQueue")
private var videoOutput =3D AVCaptureVideoDataOutput()

func setupSession() {=20
=09=09=09=09guard let video =3D AVCaptureDevice.default(.builtInDualWideCam=
era,for: .video, position: .back) else { return }
        guard let videoInput =3D try? AVCaptureDeviceInput(device: video) e=
lse { return }
          =20
        guard session.canAddInput(videoInput) else { return }
        session.addInput(videoInput)
       =20
        videoOutput.setSampleBufferDelegate(self, queue: DispatchQueue(labe=
l: "sampleBufferQueue"))
       =20
        session.addOutput(videoOutput)
}
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We'll use the camera's input and output to recognize and track the objec=
ts displayed on screen.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">override=
 func viewDidLoad() {
=09=09=09=09checkPermission()=20
        sessionQueue.async { [unowned self] in
            guard permissionGranted else { return }
            self.setupSession()
        }
 }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To detect the images being captured, it=E2=80=99s necessary to initializ=
e a model that can detect the objects we want to track. This model can be b=
uilt and trained from scratch, or we can import a pre-trained one.</p>
<p>In our example, we=E2=80=99ll import a pre-trained model and extract its=
 recognitions.</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func set=
upModel() {
        let modelURL =3D Bundle.main.url(forResource: "OurModel", withExten=
sion: "mlmodelc")
   =20
        do {
            let visionModel =3D try VNCoreMLModel(for: MLModel(contentsOf: =
modelURL!))
            let recognitions =3D VNCoreMLRequest(model: visionModel, comple=
tionHandler: detectionDidComplete)
            self.requests =3D [recognitions]
        } catch let error {
            print(error)
        }
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>To draw bounding boxes around the detected objects, we need to add a var=
iable to our controller responsible for handling the layer that highlights =
detected objects:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">var dete=
ction: CALayer! =3D nil</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Then we add this layer to the view and update it after each detection:</=
p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func set=
upLayers() {
        detection =3D CALayer()
        detection.frame =3D CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: screenRect.size.width=
, height: screenRect.size.height)
        self.view.layer.addSublayer(detection)
    }
   =20
 func updateLayers() {
        detection?.frame =3D CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: screenRect.size.widt=
h, height: screenRect.size.height)
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Now we can draw the bounding box around the detected object:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func dra=
wBoundingBox(_ bounds: CGRect) -&gt; CALayer {
        let boxLayer =3D CALayer()
        boxLayer.frame =3D bounds
        boxLayer.borderWidth =3D 3.0
        boxLayer.borderColor =3D CGColor.init(red: 0.0, green: 0.0, blue: 0=
.0, alpha: 1.0)
        boxLayer.cornerRadius =3D 4
        return boxLayer
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>We extract data from the observations generated by the model in <code>se=
tupModel</code> and update our <code>detection</code> layer to draw the bou=
nding box:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">func det=
ections(_ results: [VNObservation]) {
        detection.sublayers =3D nil
       =20
        for observation in results where observation is VNRecognizedObjectO=
bservation {
            guard let objectObservation =3D observation as? VNRecognizedObj=
ectObservation else { continue }
           =20
            let bounds =3D VNImageRectForNormalizedRect(objectObservation.b=
oundingBox, Int(screenRect.size.width), Int(screenRect.size.height))
            let transformedBounds =3D CGRect(x: bounds.minX, y: screenRect.=
size.height - bounds.maxY, width: bounds.maxX - bounds.minX, height: bounds=
.maxY - bounds.minY)
           =20
            let boxLayer =3D self.drawBoundingBox(transformedBounds)

            detection.addSublayer(boxLayer)
        }
    }
   =20
    func detectionDidComplete(request: VNRequest, error: Error?) {
        DispatchQueue.main.async(execute: {
            if let results =3D request.results {
                self.detections(results)
            }
        })
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Finally, we add the <code>setupModel</code> and <code>setupLayers</code>=
 calls to <code>viewDidLoad</code> and start the AV session:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: php; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">override=
 func viewDidLoad() {
        checkPermission()
       =20
        sessionQueue.async { [unowned self] in
            guard permissionGranted else { return }
            self.setupSession()
            self.setupModel()
            self.setupLayers()
           =20
            self.session.startRunning()
        }
    }</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>And that=E2=80=99s it: we=E2=80=99re ready to use object tracking data i=
n our app!</p>
<p>By adding checks inside the <code>detections</code> function, we can eas=
ily verify if we=E2=80=99ve achieved our desired result.</p>
<h2 id=3D"ObjectTracking-Conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>Object tracking is one of the key technologies behind many of the innova=
tive features found in Apple products, enabling more natural and intelligen=
t interactions with mobile devices.</p>
<p>From features like <strong>ARKit</strong> for augmented reality to <stro=
ng>Face ID</strong> and the <strong>camera=E2=80=99s portrait mode</strong>=
, Apple uses object tracking efficiently and accurately to enhance the user=
 experience across a variety of contexts. Through the <strong>Vision framew=
ork</strong>, Apple also provides developers with powerful tools to impleme=
nt their own object tracking solutions on iOS, opening the door to countles=
s creative and practical applications.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
